---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Frontier Research — monogate.org"
  description="Active Monogate research fronts: boundary concentration, proof-carrying rescue packets, Forge replay artifacts, and MachLib obligations."
>
  <section class="frontier-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Monogate Research</p>
    <h1>Frontier map</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The current frontier is not a single theorem. It is a repeatable loop:
      Forge emits evidence, monogate.dev inspects it, MachLib names the proof
      obligations, and monogate.org keeps the public claim boundary honest.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Active instrument</p>
    <h2>Proof-carrying rescue suite</h2>
    <p>
      The rescue suite tracks four optimizer boundary events through named
      rescue operators and replayable witness packets. The generated Explorer
      fixture is derived from the Forge manifest and replay JSON.
    </p>
    <div class="links">
      <a href="https://monogate.dev/explorer/rescue-suite">Open Explorer</a>
      <a href="/blog/proof-carrying-rescue-suite-v0">Read v0 note</a>
      <a href="/blog/how-to-read-the-rescue-suite">Read the guide</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="grid" aria-label="Frontier research fronts">
    <article>
      <p class="tag">geometry</p>
      <h2>Boundary concentration</h2>
      <p>
        High-dimensional search space pushes useful structure toward sparse
        boundary regions. This is the substrate behind corner concentration,
        phantom attractors, and rescue-triggered optimization traces.
      </p>
      <a href="/blog/why-eml-optimization-lives-on-the-boundary">Read the boundary essay</a>
    </article>

    <article>
      <p class="tag">forge</p>
      <h2>Replayable rescue packets</h2>
      <p>
        Forge now emits a suite manifest, replay result, markdown summary, and
        Explorer fixture from one command. The goal is repeatable research
        artifacts, not screenshots or hand-entered tables.
      </p>
      <code>forge rescue --suite --strict</code>
    </article>

    <article>
      <p class="tag">machlib</p>
      <h2>Proof obligations</h2>
      <p>
        The suite routes each rescue lane to a named MachLib obligation. The
        current public claim is obligation routing plus packet witnesses, not
        completed semantic-rewrite proofs.
      </p>
      <a href="/blog/proof-carrying-rescue-status">See status table</a>
    </article>

    <article>
      <p class="tag">claim boundary</p>
      <h2>What is claimed</h2>
      <p>
        We claim generated analysis artifacts, simulated traces, replay checks,
        and explicit proof-obligation names. We do not yet claim production
        optimizer rewrites, hardware observations, or fully discharged formal
        proofs for the whole suite.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="panel">
    <p class="eyebrow">Next pressure point</p>
    <h2>Move one lane from routed to discharged</h2>
    <p>
      The cleanest first target is the log-domain lane:
      <code>PositiveCoordinateObligation</code>. It is narrow, inspectable, and
      close to the mathematical heart of the rescue idea: transform invalid
      raw coordinates into a positive internal coordinate path.
    </p>
  </section>

  <style>
    .frontier-hero {
      padding: 36px 0 20px;
    }
    .eyebrow,
    .tag {
      margin: 0 0 8px;
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.68rem;
      letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--orange);
    }
    h1 {
      margin: 0 0 14px;
      font-size: 2.2rem;
      line-height: 1.1;
    }
    h2 {
      margin: 0 0 10px;
      font-size: 1.12rem;
      line-height: 1.25;
    }
    .lede,
    p {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.7;
    }
    .lede {
      max-width: 720px;
      font-size: 1.02rem;
    }
    .panel,
    article {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 8px;
      background: var(--surface);
      padding: 20px;
    }
    .grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 14px;
      margin: 18px 0;
    }
    .links {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 8px;
      margin-top: 14px;
    }
    .links a,
    article a {
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 0.82rem;
    }
    .links a {
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 6px;
      padding: 7px 10px;
      text-decoration: none;
    }
    code {
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    @media (max-width: 720px) {
      .grid {
        grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      }
      h1 {
        font-size: 1.7rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</Base>
